NAME
	libkeccak - Keccak-family hashing library

DESCRIPTION
	libkeccak is a bit-oriented lanewise implementation of
	the Keccak-family with support for extend output size,
	state marshalling, algorithm tuning with implicit parameters,
	secure erasure of sensitive data, and HMAC.

RATIONALE
	Keccak won the SHA-3 competition. We need a library for it,
	and let it be bit-oriented.

KT128 (KANGAROOTWELVE)
	libkeccak also provides a byte-oriented, streaming KT128
	(RFC 9861, 128-bit security) through the opaque
	struct libkeccak_k12 declared in libkeccak/k12.h.

	The call order is:

		libkeccak_k12_create   allocate a context
		libkeccak_k12_update   message chunks, any number, in order
		libkeccak_k12_final    customisation string; ends the input
		libkeccak_k12_squeeze  XOF bytes, any number, concatenating
		libkeccak_k12_destroy  release the context (NULL is safe)

	update/final/squeeze return 0 on success and -1 on error. Chunk
	boundaries do not affect the result, zero-length updates and reads
	are allowed, NUL bytes are ordinary data, and caller buffers are
	only borrowed for the call. final succeeds once; squeeze before it
	fails without writing. The tree decision is made over the message,
	the customisation string and the encoding of its length together,
	so an empty customisation string still contributes its one-byte
	encoding. The permutation is Keccak-p[1600,12], the last twelve
	rounds of Keccak-f[1600]; the SHA-3/SHAKE interfaces keep all 24.

	Space is bounded and independent of input length: the whole
	context and every buffer it uses occupy one fixed allocation of
	about 9 KiB and never exceed 32 KiB. The message, the
	customisation string and the piece chaining values are never
	stored in full, and update/final/squeeze perform no
	input-length-dependent allocation. See libkeccak_k12(7) and the
	program in example/.

SEE ALSO
	sha3sum, libblake(7), libsha2(7), libsha1(7), libhashsum(7)
